exercises.solution:
```python
def reduce_word(word):
    stack = []
    for step in word:
        if stack and stack[-1] == -step:
            stack.pop()
        else:
            stack.append(step)
    return tuple(stack)
```

A reduced word in one generator is $\mathsf{loop}^n$ for a unique $n \in \Z$, namely its winding number. That reduced words are classified by $\Z$ is the combinatorial shadow of $(\mathsf{base} = \mathsf{base}) \simeq \Z$; the type-theoretic proof (encode–decode) shows it for all loops, not only those built from words.
